I. Introduction
H
ELICOPTERS operating close to the ground in the presence of loose sediment in desert or dusty environments get engulfed in large dust clouds, which is also known as the “brownout” problem. Under certain operational conditions, such as takeoff and landing, an intense dust cloud can severely obscure the pilot’s vision, leading to a loss of situational awareness. In the recent past, military operations in brownout conditions have resulted in several helicopter accidents, forcing helicopter analysts to investigate the physics related to brownout and try to develop long-term mitigation strategies. The interaction of the rotor wake with the ground and the dust particles forms the root cause of the brownout problem, and a detailed understanding of the fluid physics along with a good knowledge of the fluid–particle interaction under these conditions can help
researchers develop effective means of preventing and/or mitigating the adverse effects of rotorcraft brownout. To understand the effect of rotor wake interaction with the ground in the context of the brownout problem, several experimental analyses have been performed. Most of these efforts have been with the use of laboratory/microscale rotors. On several occasions, the experiments have been conducted without the dust particles to have a better understanding of rotor wake interaction with ground. One such experimental study was performed by Lee et al. [1] using particle image velocimetry (PIV) measurements on a microscale rotor at various heights above a ground plane: 0.25R, 0.5R, 1.0R, and 1.5R, where R is the rotor radius. As the rotor wake approached the ground, vortex stretching was shown to have the effect of increasing swirl velocities within the vortex cores, leading to a reintensification of vorticity. It was found that these stretching effects were largest when the rotor hovered at an intermediate height above the ground. Beyond this height, the effects of vortex diffusion became more significant than vortex stretching. Milluzzo et al. [2] measured the evolution of a hovering subscale rotor wake in-ground effect (IGE) for various tip shapes (rectangular, swept, British Experimental Rotor Programme-like, and slotted-tip) using flow visualization and phase-averaged PIV techniques. The rotor operated at a 1R rotor height above the ground. The measurements resulted in a detailed dataset of rotor wake characteristics such as core size and swirl velocity profiles for an up to 800 deg wake age. Nathan and Green [3] studied both single- and dual-phase flowfields around a microscale rotor IGE operating at different heights above ground and at low advance ratios. Investigation using single-phase flow showed the formation of a recirculation region at low advance ratios and the formation of a ground vortex at higher advance ratios. Additional experiments conducted by Johnson et al. [4] and Sydney et al. [5] on a hovering microrotor showed the phenomenon of entrainment and upliftment of sediment particles. More recently, Glucksman-Glaser and Jones [6] used dye flow visualization and PIV techniques to qualitatively and quantitatively measure the flowfield around a microscale rotor operating over a sediment bed in a water tank. It was found that the passage of tip vortices was causing the unsteady velocities to cause sediment mobilization. These aforementioned experiments provide a detailed understanding of the fluid dynamics for the micro- and subscale rotor in-ground effect. However, the wide range of parameters affecting the brownout problem are still difficult to model in experiments. The main experimental difficulty comes in simulating the brownout conditions in
