full-scale rotors. Computational studies can be used to overcome these challenges by simulating a wide range of brownout conditions with relative ease. However, one of the challenges in computational studies is to preserve the tip vortex emanating from the blade for a significantly long time to capture their interaction with the ground. These challenges are further aggravated in terms of computational expense when the scale of the problem is increased because finer meshes are required to capture the rotor wake at higher Reynolds number. The focus of several past studies, including this one, is to concentrate on the problem of tracking the evolution of the rotor tip vortices and capture the rotor wake for longer wake ages. Over the last few years, researchers modeling the rotor wake in the presence of ground have employed aerodynamic models of various levels of sophistication. Most approaches track the tip vortex in the particle-fixed Lagrangian frame of reference [7–9]. Even though these free-vortex-type methods can track the vortex for a long distance, these methods involve a certain degree of empiricism in determining the vortex core radius and rollup. Methods using the vorticity transport model (VTM) [10] or vorticity confinement [11] provide a capability for a more fundamental representation of the formation and evolution of the tip vortex. However, VTM applications have been run as an inviscid model. On the other hand, vorticity confinement applications have used very coarse meshes that may not be sufficient to capture the details of the vortical wake. Furthermore, in these studies, the blades were treated using liftingline aerodynamics with their accompanying assumptions. Thus, in their current forms, they still retain a fair amount of empiricism. To avoid the empiricism present in the aforementioned models, several researchers have employed a Reynolds-averaged NavierStokes (RANS) methodology to capture the rotor wake in the presence of ground. Hariharan et al. [12] used a RANS-based solver, called Helios, that was capable of adaptive mesh refinement to resolve the important flow features locally along with a combination of higher-order schemes. This work used an inviscid wall boundary condition to model the ground plane; hence, the turbulence near the ground was not accurately captured. Kutz el al. [13] performed rotor IGE computations in hover and low-speed forward flight. Computations were performed using a RANS solver with the ground plane using a viscous wall boundary condition. The lack of discrete vortices was observed in the flowfield due to the lack of mesh resolution in this work. In the aforementioned efforts to use a RANS methodology, the use of fine meshes or viscous boundary conditions has been avoided to prevent computational expense. To reduce the computational expense of a RANS methodology, Thomas et al. [14] developed a hybrid methodology that used a free vortex methods (FVM) (Lagrangian model) to obtain the rotor wake and convect the vortex filaments, coupled with a RANS (Eulerian model) model close to the ground to capture the turbulence near the ground. The results showed promise in avoiding huge computational costs of resolving the rotor wake all the way to the ground; however, the use of FVM methods still leads to empiricism. All the aforementioned modeling efforts either comprised the use of empiricism to model rotor wake or did not have sufficient mesh resolution to resolve the rotor wake accurately. The current work seeks to overcome these issues using a RANS-based computational fluid dynamics (CFD) solver. The mesh resolution is provided with the use of parallelization and intelligent clustering of mesh points in regions of interest. The current work builds upon a framework described by Lakshminarayan et al. [15] to model a hovering microrotor (aspect ratio of 4.387) in ground effect with high fidelity. This work modeled the experimental work of Lee et al. [1] in IGE conditions at different rotor heights above ground and provided a highly accurate comparison of the performance and rotor wake between the experiments and simulations. The current work extends the computational framework presented in [15] to model a subscale rotor (aspect ratio of 9.132) operating at a rotor height of 1R above ground. The experiments of Milluzzo et al. [2] as described in the next section are used for validation. A novel mesh setup is formed for these high-aspect-ratio rotor blades to provide a balance between the available computational resources without sacrificing the accuracy of
capturing a rotor wake for longer wake ages. New challenges with the RANS methodology are observed and hybrid RANS/large-eddy simulation (LES) methods are explored that provide good agreement with the available subscale rotor experiments [2]. The following section describes the experimental setup for the subscale rotor blade.
II. Experimental Setup for Validation
The experimental setup of Milluzzo et al. [2] shown in Fig. 1 is used to validate the flowfield of a hovering one-bladed rotor operating in ground effect. The motivation of the experimental work to use a one-bladed rotor hovering in ground effect stems from focusing on the generation and convection of the rotor wake, for which the properties can be more accurately measured until it interacts with the ground with fewer blades. This allows for a better understanding of aperiodicity of the rotor wake at older wake ages. The rotor consisted of an untwisted rectangular blade with a NACA 2415 airfoil. The rotor blade had a chord length of 44.5 mm and a radius of 0.408 m, resulting in an aspect ratio of 9.132. The blades were operated at a collective pitch of 4.5 deg. The rotor operated at a tip Mach number of 0.24 and a chord Reynolds number of 250,000. The rotor blades operated at a rotor height of 1R above the ground. The experiments were performed on four different blades obtained by varying the blade tip shapes. In this work, only the rectangular tip blade is modeled to understand the importance of length scale selection in turbulence models for preserving vorticity in sections below.
III. Computational Setup: Flow Solver
In the present work, the RANS-based solver OVERTURNS [16] is used to perform the computations in a time-accurate manner in an inertial frame of reference. The code solves the compressible RANS equations using a preconditioned dual-time scheme. The secondorder backward differencing scheme is used for time marching, and the linear system is solved using the lower–upper symmetric GaussSeidel [17] framework. Low Mach preconditioning [18] is used to improve the convergence and accuracy of the flow solver. A thirdorder MUSCL scheme [19] using Koren’s limiter [20] is used for spatial reconstruction on the blade and the main background mesh, whereas a fifth-order mapped, weighted essentially nonoscillatory scheme [21] scheme is used for the vortex-tracking grids and the funnel-shaped mesh. More details about the meshes are provided in the following section. The Roe flux difference scheme [22] is used for calculating inviscid fluxes, and a second-order central scheme is used to discretize the viscous terms. The Spalart–Allmaras [23] turbulence model is employed for the RANS closure. The production term in this eddy viscosity model is modified to account for the reduction in
