IV. Computational Setup: Mesh System
The wake capturing is initially performed by using a blade mesh and a single cylindrical background mesh. The blade mesh comprises a body-conforming C-O type mesh (Fig. 2a). The blade mesh consists of 267 × 185 × 50 points in the streamwise, spanwise, and wallnormal directions. One-hundred and eighty-seven mesh points are along the airfoil surface in the streamwise direction. A spacing of 5.0 × 105 chords is used in the wall-normal direction, which corresponds to a y value of around 1.0. The background mesh (Fig. 2b) is a cylindrical Cartesian mesh that consists of identical planes rotated in the azimuthal direction. Each azimuthal plane is spaced at every 1.5 deg in azimuth. A cross-sectional view of the background mesh is shown in (Fig. 3a). In these most refined regions, the background mesh has a grid spacing of 0.04 chords in both the radial and vertical directions. The mesh extends to 10.0R in the radial direction. The total number of mesh points in the background cylindrical mesh is 247 × 207 × 205. The simulation is performed for 15 revolutions. After 15 revolutions, the flowfield is well developed.
It is then used to obtain the instantaneous tip vortex trajectory by searching for the maximum vorticity magnitude corresponding to the vortex center at all wake ages. Once the tip vortex trajectory is obtained, the background mesh is modified to follow the path of the rotor wake to reduce the number of mesh points in the radial direction. The resulting modified background mesh is shown in Fig. 3b and is referred to as the main background mesh. In addition to the strategic refinement of the main background mesh, a vortex tracking grid (VTG), seen in Fig. 4a, is used to better the preserve the tip vortices along the path of the rotor wake. The VTG is a threedimensional helical mesh, which is generated by azimuthally extruding a two-dimensional (2-D) Cartesian plane along the instantaneous tip vortex trajectory. These 2-D planes are placed at every 1.0 deg of the azimuth, which is two-thirds the azimuthal distance in the background mesh. The 2-D planar section of the VTG is a Cartesian mesh with equispaced mesh points near the middle, which stretches at the edges to reduce the cell size disparity between the VTG and the main background mesh. The extent of the 2-D plane is calculated by accounting for the expected wandering of the tip vortex. At the edge of the VTG, the cell spacing is equal to that in the new background mesh; whereas at the center, the cell spacing is approximately one-fourth. If the VTG is obtained by using an instantaneous tip vortex trajectory, the resulting grid contains irregularities along the azimuth direction. These irregularities are damped out by using a Laplacian-based smoothing.
