V. Computational Setup Parameters and Costs
The chosen time step corresponds to 0.25 of azimuth for all the cases. A constant Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy number is maintained along with a pseudotime-step size of 10 for the Newton subiterations to provide stability throughout the iterations. In addition, at each time step, six subiterations are used to remove linearization errors. The preconditioning Mach number is set to the local Mach number, which is contrary to the tip Mach number used for the microscale rotor simulations as described by Lakshminarayan et al. [15]. This is seen to improve convergence for the subscale rotor cases. Each simulation is run for a total of 19 revolutions. The first 15 revolutions only use the modified main background mesh and the funnel mesh, and then the vortex tracking grids are added for another four revolutions. The VTGs are modified at each revolution during these last four revolutions. The calculations take about 6.25 days to complete 19 revolutions (i.e., 3.04 revolutions per day) when run in parallel on
32 AMD Interlagos Opteron 2.3 GHz processors. A total of 240 processors were used in each of these simulations.
VI. Results
Four different cases are discussed in the following section in order to understand the importance of appropriate turbulent length scaling for the subscale rotor simulations. The baseline case involves the use of RANS methodology previously used by Lakshminarayan et al. [15] successfully for the microscale rotor.
VII. Case 1: Baseline Case with S–A Model
The baseline case consists of the RANS-based solver with the Spalart–Allmaras (S–A) model to model the subscale rotor. The experimental value of thrust coefficient CT is 0.0018. The computational
prediction with the baseline S–A turbulence model predicts a CT of 0.0019. The computed power coefficient CP is 0.00012. The experiments do not predict a CP value. Figure 6a shows the comparison of the experimental measurements and computational results for the variation of the peak swirl velocity with wake age. The peak swirl velocity is nondimensionalized by the tip speed of the rotor blade. The experiments show a decrease in the peak swirl velocity until a wake age of 360 deg. This is followed by a slight increase in the peak swirl velocity due to the in-ground effect. Compared to the experiments, the computations show a rapid decrease in peak swirl velocity with increasing wake age. Even at an early wake age of 180 deg, the peak swirl velocity predicted by the computations is twice as low as the experimental values. Figure 6b shows the variation of the vortex core radius as a function of wake age for the different tip shapes. The core radius is nondimensionalized by the rotor blade chord. It can be noticed that the core radius grows much faster in the computations as compared to the experimental results. For instance, the core radius at the 720 wake age in the computations grows to an order of 0.3 chords, whereas the experiment only has a core size on the order 0.075 chords. The results from the peak swirl velocity and core radius values show that the tip vortices are not being captured well, even at early
