implies that the turbulence model (i.e., the Spalart–Allmaras turbulence model with rotational correction) being used in the RANS equations to solve for the eddy viscosity values is leading to an excessive diffusion of the vortex core. It is a known fact that the RANS equations with the Spalart–Allmaras turbulence model were formulated to predict attached or thin shear-layer flows well. This causes modeling limitations when predicting flows that involve large amounts of flow separation. As seen above, the CFD predicted rotor wake for the sub scale rotor is diffused at early wake ages. To identify that the source of dissipation of vortices is the use of turbulence modeling, simulations are performed with the assumption of laminar flow i.e. (turbulence model is not used). The hypothesis of the laminar flow assumptionbased simulations is to avoid the high turbulence levels and show a dependency on the choice of turbulence modeling in preserving the rotor wake without any increase in mesh resolution. The laminar flow simulations are performed on the wake capturing meshes (modified background mesh, vortex tracking grids, and funnel mesh). The blade mesh still solves for turbulent parameters using the S–A turbulence model. The results from the revised computations with laminar flow assumption on wake capturing meshes form case 2, as described in the following.
