VIII. Case 2: Laminar Flow Assumption on Wake-Capturing Meshes
The simulations are performed with the laminar flow assumption on the wake capturing meshes as described previously. The values of CT and CP do not change as compared to case 1. Figure 8a compares the peak swirl velocity from case 2 with a laminar flow assumption with varying wake age. In the previous computations (i.e., case 1) with the turbulence model being used in all the meshes, the peak swirl velocity decreases at a high rate and reaches very low values at older wake ages. However, in case 2 with the laminar flow assumption on the wake capturing meshes, the peak swirl velocity values look in good agreement with the experiments. Figure 8b shows the core radius growth from case 2 and is compared with the experiments. The computations in case 2 show a close agreement with the experiments. This shows that the assumption of laminar flow in wake capturing meshes is preserving the vortex for longer wake ages. Consequently, these results confirm that the amount of turbulence predicted by the use of S–A model in case 1 were leading to the excessive diffusion of the tip vortices. It can also be noticed that the revised computations with laminar assumption on the wake capturing meshes show a smaller core radius growth than the experiments due to the lack of any physical turbulence. This also indicates that the use of
a laminar flow assumption might be overpredicting the vortex strength in case 2 as compared to the experiments. More important, the experimental work (Milluzzo et al. [2], Johnson et al. [4], and Sydney et al. [5]) to study brownout indicates that the entrainment of sediment during brownout is caused by the viscous interaction of the rotor wake and ground. This includes the presence of discrete tip vortices trailed from rotor blades as well as secondary flows from a vortex pairing that create an unsteady aerodynamic environment near the ground, causing the sediment entrainment. This necessitates the accurate prediction of turbulence levels at the ground plane for an accurate understanding of the physical phenomenon affecting the mobilization of the particles during brownout.
IX. Case 3: Delayed Detached-Eddy Simulations, Use of a RANS/LES Approach
Based on the previous two cases of computations, it is established that the use of a turbulence model in its current form is the cause of excessive diffusion of the tip vortices. Moreover, accurate prediction of turbulence levels is required in the computations in order to understand the interaction of the rotor wake with the ground plane with ramifications to the brownout phenomenon. It is a known fact that the RANS equations were formulated to predict attached or thin shear-layer flows well. This causes modeling limitations using the RANS equations when predicting flows that involve large amounts of flow separation. Therefore, one needs to investigate the use of a higher-fidelity approach such as large-eddy simulation for this problem. Because the LES approach is seen to be prohibitively expensive for practical engineering applications, one way to use a higher level of approximation to account for turbulence above that in RANS is to use a hybrid approach between LES and RANS. In a hybrid approach, the boundary-layer/near-wall regions (small-scale turbulence) are modeled using RANS, and regions of massive separation (large-scale turbulence) are resolved using LES. The existing RANS methodology can be extended to include the hybrid approach by modifying the distance function in the S–A model. The modified distance function is based on grid spacing to provide the switch between the RANS and LES limits. Accordingly, a grid spacing-based limiter approach was developed by Spalart et al. [27] and is referred to as the detached-eddy simulation (DES) [27]. To apply DES, a simple modification to the distance function d is made in the S–A turbulence model that depends upon both the wall distance and the local grid spacing. The modified distance function is as follows:
