XI. Conclusions
The compressible Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes solver OVERTURNS was used to simulate a hovering one-bladed subscale rotor operating at a tip Mach number of 0.24 and a tip Reynolds number of 250,000 in-ground effect with the primary objective of capturing the rotor wake accurately for longer wake ages. An earlier framework developed by Lakshminarayan et al. [15] showed a good agreement for the microscale rotor. However, the same framework could not be directly applied to the subscale rotor with the available computational resources. Therefore, the mesh system required novel strategies to efficiently capture the rotor wake. The simulations were done using a system of overset meshes that were strategically placed to accurately capture the formation and evolution of the tip vortex to sufficiently long duration. The results with the RANS solver showed a smeared flowfield and a DES-based approach using an appropriate formulation of the length scale results in accurate flowfield predictions. The following are specific observations/conclusions that can be drawn from the present work: 1) Different cases with different choices of modifications to the turbulence modeling did not change the thrust predictions. This implied that the thrust and power computations were independent from the choice of turbulence modeling. 2) The flowfield generated by RANS simulations showed a highly smeared-out rotor wake at early wake ages. The increased diffusion of tip vortices was attributed to the excessive turbulence levels. This could be observed by looking at the turbulent vortex cores obtained in the RANS simulations. 3) The simulations performed with laminar assumption on wake capturing grids showed a better agreement with the available experiments. However, the lack of turbulence in the flowfield also resulted in artificial preservation of tip vortices, and that led to an overestimation of the vortex strengths in computations as compared with the experiments. 4) The computations were repeated with a hybrid RANS/LES approach referred to as DDES. DDES was seen to marginally improve the CFD predictions from the original RANS methodology. 5) DDES with a modification to the length scale using the correction for anisotropic grids resulted in preserving the flowfield well, and the results agreed well with the experiments. The resulting turbulence levels showed laminar vortex cores, unlike in the RANS simulations.
